rooms_map = '''<header class="page-hero image-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Beginner route guide · version 1.0</p><h1>Garden of Witches Map: Rooms, Rewards &amp; Routes</h1><p>Understand what changes between runs, read the reward before entering, and choose a route that keeps one working build alive.</p></div><img src="../assets/media/steam-screenshot-05.jpg" width="600" height="338" alt="Sil fighting a group of enemies in a Garden of Witches combat room"></header><article class="article-layout"><div class="prose"><section class="direct-answer"><b>Direct answer</b><p>Garden of Witches does not generate every room from scratch. The developer describes authored combat rooms whose <strong>placement and clear rewards are randomized</strong>. The map shows the reward type before entry, so choose by current health, loadout fit and risk instead of memorizing one path.</p></section><section class="quick-answer"><h2>At every door</h2><ol><li>Read the visible reward.</li><li>Check whether health can support the risk.</li><li>Ask which equipped action the reward improves.</li><li>Keep one path toward recovery or a safer fight when available.</li><li>Commit knowing the route moves forward.</li></ol></section><h2 id="random">What is random and what stays authored?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Part of a run</th><th>What to expect</th><th>Beginner consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Combat room layout</td><td>Rooms are authored rather than assembled from random tiles</td><td>A repeated room can reward learned positioning</td></tr><tr><td>Room placement</td><td>The same authored room can appear in a different place</td><td>Do not memorize one route order</td></tr><tr><td>Clear reward</td><td>Rewards can differ and the map previews the type</td><td>Read the icon before choosing</td></tr><tr><td>Enemies in a known room</td><td>The developer's explanation says a repeated room keeps its enemy setup</td><td>Use prior failures to learn the room</td></tr><tr><td>Domain</td><td>Different witch domains use their own enemy sets</td><td>Expect the learning problem to change with progression</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This explanation came from a Team Tapas community manager before 1.0. The full release changed combat systems and reward categories, so use it for the map's structural model, not for an old list of reward names. The current map legend and tooltip decide the exact 1.0 label.</p><h2 id="choose">Choose the next room with a four-question filter</h2><h3>1. Can health survive the next mistake?</h3><p>When health is low enough that one ordinary error ends the chapter attempt, recovery is the build upgrade. A powerful Imprint has no value if Sil cannot reach the next major fight. Treat health as permission to take risk, not as a number to ignore until the boss.</p><h3>2. Does the reward support an action you already land?</h3><p>A beginner build becomes coherent when several choices improve the same repeatable action. For the default Scissors, that may mean creating a safer full-combo opening. For a charged weapon, it means enough space and direction to release. A reward tied to a missing trigger is not synergy; it is a promise the current loadout cannot keep.</p><h3>3. Is the room teaching the same failure again?</h3><p>If the prior rooms interrupted every combo, another pure damage reward does not solve the real issue. Choose control, movement, range or recovery that changes the failure. When a repeated authored room appears, use the same opening with one adjustment and compare the result.</p><h3>4. What will you need before the major fight?</h3><p>Do not spend the last safe movement option or replace a familiar skill immediately before a durable target. A route is successful when it reaches the fight with a readable loop, enough health and one exit action. Peak damage in an easy room is not the objective.</p><h2 id="types">Reward decisions by job, not color</h2><div class="table-wrap troubleshooting"><table><thead><tr><th>Reward job</th><th>Take it when</th><th>Avoid it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recovery</td><td>Health blocks the next risky room or fight</td><td>The run is healthy and another choice fixes a known weakness</td></tr><tr><td>Imprint / behavior change</td><td>It improves the equipped skill's actual input loop</td><td>It replaces a loop you have not learned just before a hard room</td></tr><tr><td>Relic connection</td><td>The loadout already creates the stated trigger</td><td>It depends on a skill, state or resource you do not have</td></tr><tr><td>Control or movement</td><td>Attacks fail because enemies interrupt or leave range</td><td>The current action is already safe and only output is lacking</td></tr><tr><td>Higher-risk encounter</td><td>Health, recovery and the core loop are stable</td><td>The last room exposed an unresolved survival problem</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2 id="stuck">If the route choice went wrong</h2><p>Do not rebuild every slot. Name the first failure: low health, interrupted input, missing target coverage or an unusable reward trigger. Change the next choice that addresses that failure. If the run ends, return home and preserve the lesson. The next map will differ, but the decision rule still works.</p><p>When a guide presents exact room probabilities or a universal route without a current capture, treat it as provisional. The useful fact is visible in your run: the reward shown, the health available and the action the build can actually repeat.</p><div class="source-list"><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://steamcommunity.com/app/2530470/discussions/0/524225618711368430/" rel="nofollow noopener">Team Tapas community manager explanation of authored rooms and randomized placement/rewards</a></li><li><a href="https://steamcommunity.com/app/2530470/allnews/" rel="nofollow noopener">Official 1.0 announcements and current system boundary</a></li></ul></div><div class="related-reading"><h2>Continue the beginner route</h2><a href="getting-started.html">First-run checklist</a><a href="controls.html">Fix input problems</a><a href="armory.html">Prepare a coherent loadout</a><a href="../walkthrough.html">Follow story checkpoints</a></div>''' + faq_html(rooms_map_faq) + '''</div><aside class="toc"><b>On this page</b><a href="#random">What changes</a><a href="#choose">Four-question filter</a><a href="#types">Reward jobs</a><a href="#stuck">Wrong route</a></aside></article>'''

upgrades_reset_faq = [
    ("Do upgrades reset in Garden of Witches?", "Temporary Imprints, Relics and other run rewards reset with the run. Permanent level-based upgrades selected in Sil's House should persist on the same save."),
    ("Why did my permanent upgrade level return to zero?", "Players reported this after the 1.0 launch, and the Team Tapas community manager treated it as a bug investigation. Preserve the save, capture before/after screens and check the latest known-bugs thread."),
    ("Was any upgrade reset intentional?", "An older 0.9 system migration intentionally reset prior upgrades so players could reselect them with the new level system. That one-time migration is different from current 1.0 permanent progress disappearing during normal play."),
]
upgrades_reset = '''<header class="page-hero image-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">Beginner progression help · version 1.0</p><h1>Garden of Witches Upgrades Reset? Progress Guide</h1><p>Tell a normal roguelike run reset from missing permanent Tapestry progress, then preserve the evidence needed for a real save bug.</p></div><img src="../assets/media/steam-screenshot-02.jpg" width="600" height="338" alt="Garden of Witches ability and progression interface"></header><article class="article-layout"><div class="prose"><section class="direct-answer"><b>Direct answer</b><p><strong>In-run power resets normally:</strong> the Imprints, Relics and reward stack assembled during one attempt do not become the next run's starting build. <strong>Permanent house upgrades should persist:</strong> if spent Tapestry nodes or character level return to zero on the same save, current 1.0 reports treat that as a bug, not intended progression.</p></section><section class="quick-answer"><h2>Do this before playing more</h2><ol><li>Do not overwrite or delete the affected save.</li><li>Open the same save slot and photograph the level and Tapestry state.</li><li>Record the last chapter transition and whether another slot was loaded.</li><li>Check the pinned known-bugs thread and the current game version.</li><li>Report the before/after state instead of grinding replacement points.</li></ol></section><h2 id="difference">What resets and what should persist?</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Progress type</th><th>Expected behavior</th><th>How to recognize it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imprints</td><td>Temporary run build</td><td>Chosen as rewards that change equipped skills during the attempt</td></tr><tr><td>Relics and run rewards</td><td>Temporary run build</td><td>Assembled from the current route's reward choices</td></tr><tr><td>Room order and reward offers</td><td>Can change between runs</td><td>The map presents a new placement or reward path</td></tr><tr><td>Unlocked Scissors and Spells</td><td>Permanent progression choices</td><td>Available again through the Armory on the progressing save</td></tr><tr><td>Level and spent house upgrade nodes</td><td>Permanent on the same save</td><td>Visible in the level-based progression / Tapestry interface</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The exact menu label can differ by version or localization. The practical distinction is where the choice belongs. If it was earned and selected as part of one chapter attempt, expect the run to rebuild. If it belongs to the save's house progression, it should not silently vanish after loading the same slot.</p><h2 id="migration">The older intentional reset is not the same issue</h2><p>During the 0.9 system overhaul, Team Tapas replaced achievement-and-gold progression with a level-based system. The official notes warned that the migration would reset existing upgrades and return the new currency so players could select nodes again. That was a planned one-time conversion tied to a major update.</p><p>Version 1.0 launch reports describe a different symptom: players finishing Chapter 1, entering Chapter 2 or loading a slot and then seeing level or Tapestry upgrades return to zero. In the public thread, the Team Tapas community manager asked for save files and screenshots and escalated the issue to the developers. Do not explain that symptom away as normal roguelike design.</p><h2 id="diagnose">Five checks that protect the save</h2><ol><li><strong>Confirm the slot.</strong> Compare its timestamp and chapter with the slot used before the reset.</li><li><strong>Separate spent nodes from unspent points.</strong> Zero available points can mean they were spent; an empty node tree is a different symptom.</li><li><strong>Record the transition.</strong> Note whether the change appeared after a chapter clear, restart, game relaunch or loading another slot.</li><li><strong>Capture both screens.</strong> Include character level and the permanent upgrade interface. If an earlier screenshot exists, keep it unedited.</li><li><strong>Preserve the installation state.</strong> Record the displayed game version before updating, reinstalling or testing another branch.</li></ol><h2 id="report">A useful bug report</h2><div class="table-wrap troubleshooting"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to include</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Game version</td><td>The version visible when the reset occurred</td><td>Separates launch behavior from a later hotfix</td></tr><tr><td>Save slot</td><td>Slot number, chapter and timestamp</td><td>Rules out opening a different progression state</td></tr><tr><td>Before state</td><td>Level, spent nodes and available points</td><td>Shows what permanent progress existed</td></tr><tr><td>Trigger sequence</td><td>Clear, return home, change chapter, quit or load</td><td>Gives developers a reproduction path</td></tr><tr><td>After state</td><td>Same screens after the reset</td><td>Distinguishes UI display from lost progress</td></tr><tr><td>Save file request</td><td>Follow the developer's current support instructions</td><td>Lets the team inspect the actual state safely</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not publish or upload save files to an unrelated public site. Use the official support path requested by Team Tapas. Save files can contain account- or device-linked details, and a public mirror does not help the developer reproduce the problem.</p><h2 id="continue">What can you safely do meanwhile?</h2><p>Keep the affected save untouched until you have a copy or official guidance. If you continue playing, use a separate slot and record that choice. Avoid spending hours replacing permanent points before checking whether a hotfix or recovery instruction exists. Normal run learning—controls, room decisions and weapon practice—can continue on another slot without pretending the missing progression is expected.</p><p>If only Imprints, Relics or the route's temporary rewards disappeared after defeat, no recovery is required. Start the next run with a coherent Armory baseline, then rebuild from the new map. The reset guide exists to prevent these two very different cases from being confused.</p><div class="source-list"><h3>Sources</h3><ul><li><a href="https://steamcommunity.com/app/2530470/discussions/0/590687864233367316/" rel="nofollow noopener">Version 1.0 player reports and Team Tapas bug investigation</a></li><li><a href="https://steamcommunity.com/app/2530470/allnews/" rel="nofollow noopener">Official progression-system and 1.0 announcements</a></li></ul></div><div class="related-reading"><h2>Continue safely</h2><a href="getting-started.html">First-run guide</a><a href="rooms-and-map.html">Map and room choices</a><a href="armory.html">Armory and permanent unlocks</a><a href="../walkthrough.html">Chapter save checkpoints</a></div>''' + faq_html(upgrades_reset_faq) + '''</div><aside class="toc"><b>On this page</b><a href="#difference">What resets</a><a href="#migration">Old migration</a><a href="#diagnose">Protect the save</a><a href="#report">Bug report</a><a href="#continue">Continue safely</a></aside></article>'''

